From 962d080c76eb505dfab08465754f91aa30ab35cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:07:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [GStreamer] Let a video sink name the dma-heap it imports
 from

A decoder that writes its pictures into dma-bufs has to allocate them
from a dma-heap whose buffers the element showing the frames can import:
a display controller without an IOMMU needs physically contiguous
memory, other importers take the system heap. Only the sink side can
tell. Add a GstContext, webkit.dma-heap, through which a video sink
names that heap by its /dev/dma_heap path together with the number of
frames it holds before it lets one go, and an allocator for the heap
whose buffers stay mapped for their lifetime, since a pool maps each of
them once per frame and mapping a heap buffer remaps all of its pages.
The context reaches the sink before the decoder is linked to it, so a
pool can be sized from its first allocation on. The GL video sink
answers the context with the first heap, the system heap first, from
which the shared EGL display imports a test buffer, and reports as held
frames, in the context and in allocation queries, its queue, the appsink
and the two frames the compositor keeps. The player asks its video sink
for the context on behalf of an element that is not linked to the sink
yet.

